Ambassador Jeff Bartos
U.S. Representative for U.N. Management and Reform
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
The United States thanks Pakistan for convening today’s Arria-formula meeting.
Colleagues, when constructed properly, international agreements serve important purposes such as outlining obligations between sovereign states, preventing disputes and protecting shared interests.
The U.S. National Security Strategy is clear that U.S. policy is no longer tied to a network of international institutions and agreements, some of which are driven by outright anti-Americanism and many by transnationalism that explicitly seeks to dissolve individual state sovereignty.
As Secretary of State Rubio has noted, what started as a pragmatic framework of international organizations and agreements for peace and cooperation has morphed into a sprawling architecture of global governance, often dominated by progressive ideology and detached from national interests.
That is why, for example, the United States is taking the required steps to withdraw from the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change, the convention which forms the legal foundation for the Paris Climate Agreement. The United States has also informed the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change that we will no longer participate.
The U.S. approach to international agreements and organizations is not one of slash and burn – rather, it is one of careful recalibration.
In closing, the United States is ushering in a new era of peace through strength. Strength can enable the achievement of peace because parties that respect U.S. strength often seek our help and are receptive to our efforts to resolve conflicts and maintain peace.
Thank you.
