SEAN HANNITY: Not that it really matters, but the Human Rights Office at the UN is really upset that the U.S. apprehended Venezuela’s brutal, illegitimate dictator and drug trafficker Nicolas Maduro. Sure, he caused immense suffering, stole money from his own people, caused a nationwide famine, ignored the results of an election, trafficked drugs, killed political opponents, and murdered peaceful protesters, locked up political dissidents. But according to the UN’s Human Rights Office, the world is now less safe with Nicolas Maduro out of power. Every year our country, this country, gives between $13 and $20 billion to the UN. Why?
Here now, the U.S. Ambassador to the UN, Michael Waltz. Mike, we’ve been friends a long time. I don’t mean any offense by this. I’d like to see you out of a job, and the United States finally stopped contributing all that money, and moved the UN out of the U.S., because you have a Human Rights Council that often puts countries like, oh, Iran and Iraq on the board, and they have historically been anti-American and anti-Israel.
AMBASSADOR MIKE WALTZ: Well, Sean, I’m proud to report, under this President, we defunded the so-called Human Rights Council, and we just pushed and pressed the UN to agree to cut over 3000 of its bureaucrats, its first budget cut in its history. So, we’re doge-ing away. And the irony on this report is they actually did some decent work down in Venezuela, highlighting, documenting all of the political prisoners, the people that were being executed, the people that were being tortured by Maduro. But now I guess they just want some more strongly worded letters.
Well, President Trump took real action. The world is safer, stronger and certainly more prosperous, because of it. And I don’t know why they’re so upset. As I pointed out at the Security Council, over 50 countries didn’t even recognize Maduro, because he stole the election. So, it’s hard to figure out what they’re so upset about. Well, I think we know the answer. It’s because it’s President Trump, and he’s a man of action, and took real action. And now the Venezuelan people are going to be better off. I pointed out when the United States took action years ago, the Panamanian people, because Noriega was arrested, convicted, served time in both the United States, France, and Panama, are now better off. Look, at the end of the day, this was the right thing to do. President Trump took bold action, and we’ll continue to swing and doge that ridiculous organization called the United Nations.
HANNITY: All right, Ambassador. Thanks for keeping us up to speed. Thanks for your hard work, and I’m sure you have a lot of thoughtful things to share with your colleagues there.
